\section{Troubleshooting}{

\strong{Upgrading rlang invalidates the gargle token cache.} gargle names the
OAuth client \verb{<project_id>_<hash>}, where the hash comes from
\code{rlang::hash()}, and that function's output changed in rlang 1.3.0. A token
minted under the older rlang carries the old name, so a session running the
newer rlang does not find it. An interactive session hides this, because it
offers the cached tokens rather than matching them strictly.

To fix it:
\enumerate{
\item Start a fresh R session, so the rlang loaded is the one installed.
Re-authenticating from a session started before the upgrade writes the
token under the old name again.
\item Move the stale token file this function names out of the cache.
\item Run \code{\link[=mc_auth]{mc_auth()}} and complete the browser flow.
\item Run \code{mc_auth_check()} again from a non-interactive session, e.g.
\verb{Rscript -e 'mc::mc_auth_check()'}.
